|~ `::tagged`, the kind of every value under a tag, which the walk of a
   tagged value reaches after its own tags, and what is declared under
   it: the words of tagged values. ~|

:tagged
  |~~ A value under a tag is a payload read by the kind its tag names,
      `::Box[1 2]`, `::Box{:a 1}`, `::Box(5)`, `::Box"s"`: the tag says
      how to read the data beneath, and the data keeps its shape, so the
      words of the payload's kind still serve it, `::Box[1 2] | count`
      answering `2`. Tags stack and read from the outside in,
      `::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type` answering `::Box`.

      It is written as the tag touching its payload, whatever brackets
      hold the payload, or made by `tag`, `[1 2] | tag ::Box` answering
      `::Box[1 2]`. A tag declared as a kind runs its constructor and
      its invariants over the payload, and one nothing declares lays
      itself as it is. `payload` takes the outer tag off, and
      `within ~(…)` edits the payload under the tag, which checks it
      again. JSON writes it as an object of its `$tag` and its
      `payload`.

      A verb finds a value under a tag through its tags from the outside
      in, then this kind, then the kind of its payload, so every value
      under a tag reaches the words of this kind, a reader's kind adds
      words over the core's, and a vector under a tag stays a vector.
      ::snippet~(::tagged | manifest) lists its words.
      ::link~(::tag | doc) ::link~(::construct | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:tagged/reachesPayload
  |~~ A box answers `payload`. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])

:tagged/isNoStep
  |~~ The step `::Box[1 2]` is a `::construct`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | type | eq ::construct)

:tagged/payload
  |~~ Answers the value beneath the tag of the subject, `::Box[42] |
      payload` answering `[42]`: the reverse of `tag`, so `value | tag
      ::Foo | payload` answers the value, whatever its kind. A value
      under two tags opens one, `::A::B[1] | payload` answering
      `::B[1]`. A set is a vector under its tag, so its payload is the
      vector of its elements in their one order, `#[2 1] | payload`
      answering `[1 2]`, and a value turned by `desc` opens to the value
      it turned. A value under no tag, a number, a string, a vector or a
      plain map, has no payload and is refused. `within` edits the
      payload and puts the tag back.
      ::link~(::any/tag | doc) ::link~(::tagged/within | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:tagged/payload/answer
  |~~ The value beneath the tag, of any kind. ~~|
  ::any

:tagged/payload/SubjectNotTaggedError
  |~~ `payload` opens a value under a tag, `::Box[42] | payload`; a
      number, a string, a vector or a plain map stands under none and is
      its own value, and the kind of a value is read by `type`.
  ~~|

:tagged/payload/answersAVector
  |~~ A box of a vector answers the vector. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])

:tagged/payload/answersAString
  |~~ A note of a string answers the string. ~~|
  ::law~(:Note |~~ A note. ~~| | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")

:tagged/payload/undoesTag
  |~~ A value laid under a tag is its payload. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | tag ::Foo | payload | eq {:a 1})

:tagged/payload/opensOneTagOfTwo
  |~~ A value under two tags opens one. ~~|
  ::law~(::A::B[1] | payload | eq ::B[1])

:tagged/payload/answersTheVectorOfASet
  |~~ A set's payload is its vector in its one order. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])

:tagged/payload/SubjectNotTaggedError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number under no tag has no payload. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | payload !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/payload/SubjectNotTaggedError ::number])

:tagged/within
  |~~ Answers the subject with code run beneath its tag: the quote the
      call gives runs against the payload, as `apply` runs it, and its answer
      goes back under the tag, `::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse)` answering
      `::Box[2 1]`. The tag's constructor runs once, on the answer of the
      code, so the steps between may break what the tag holds of its
      payload: a set's constructor puts the vector the code answered in
      order, `#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0])` answering `#[1 3]`, and a
      tag whose invariant the answer breaks refuses it. The names the
      code binds stay inside it. Under two tags the code runs beneath the
      outer one, and the value beneath both is reached by nesting
      `within`, `within ~(within ~(reverse))`. The code is a quote: a
      value computed at the call, `within (reverse)`, is refused under
      the part. A value under no tag runs its code by `apply`, and is
      refused here.
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc) ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:tagged/within/answer
  |~~ The value edited beneath its tag, under the tag again. ~~|
  ::tagged

:tagged/within/code
  |~~ The edit, a quote run against the payload. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:tagged/within/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The edit `within` runs is a quote, `within ~(reverse)`; a value
      computed at the call is refused, the error a step answered there
      among them.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:tagged/within/code/NotGivenError
  |~~ `within` runs the code its call gives beneath the tag, `::Box[1 2] |
      within ~(reverse)`; a call with no code is refused.
  ~~|

:tagged/within/SubjectNotTaggedError
  |~~ `within` edits a value beneath its tag; a value under no tag, a
      vector or a plain map, is edited by `apply`, `[1 2] | apply
      ~(reverse)`.
  ~~|

:tagged/within/keepsTheTag
  |~~ The edit returns under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])

:tagged/within/editsASetAsItsVector
  |~~ Code runs on a set as on its vector, and its answer comes back a set. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])

:tagged/within/runsTheConstructorOnTheAnswer
  |~~ An answer the tag's invariant refuses is refused as it goes back under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| | :Pos/NotPositiveError ::holds~(gt 0)
    | ::Pos(3) | within ~(sub 5) !| type | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)

:tagged/within/keepsItsNamesInside
  |~~ A name the code binds stays inside it. ~~|
  ::law~(:n 7 | ::Box[1] | within ~(:n 5 | [n]) | n | eq 7)

:tagged/within/reachesUnderTwoTags
  |~~ An edit under two tags reaches the innermost payload. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~([/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])

:tagged/within/code/NotQuoteError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1] | within 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/within/code/NotQuoteError ::number])

:tagged/within/SubjectNotTaggedError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector under no tag is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | within ~(reverse)
    !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/within/SubjectNotTaggedError ::vec])

:tagged/within/code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCode
  |~~ A call with no code is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1] | within !| type | eq ::tagged/within/code/NotGivenError)

:tagged/within/checksTheAnswerAlone
  |~~ The steps between may break the tag's invariant, and the answer alone is checked. ~~|
  ::law~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| | :Pos/NotPositiveError ::holds~(gt 0) | ::Pos(3) | within ~(sub 5 | add 10) | eq ::Pos(8))
